Since you're on .NET 10, you should use the **built-in `TimeProvider`** class — no custom wrapper needed.

Here's what to do:

## 1. Register `TimeProvider` in DI

In `Program.cs` (or your DI setup):

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

## 2. Inject it into `ReportGenerator`

Replace `DateTime.UtcNow` with `timeProvider.GetUtcNow()`:

```csharp
public class ReportGenerator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var timestamp = timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("yyyyMMdd_HHmmss");
        // ...
        var content = $"Report: {reportName}\nGenerated: {timeProvider.GetUtcNow():O}\n...";
        // ...
    }
}
```

## 3. Test with `FakeTimeProvider`

Install the testing helper:

```
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

Then in tests:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing;

var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 18, 12, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var generator = new ReportGenerator(fakeTime);
var path = generator.GenerateReport("Sales");

Assert.Contains("20260718_120000", path); // deterministic!
```

You can also advance time with `fakeTime.Advance(TimeSpan.FromHours(1))`.

---

**Note:** Your `ReportGenerator` also uses `Environment.*`, `File.*`, `Directory.*`, and `Console.*` — those will need separate abstractions (`System.IO.Abstractions` for file/directory, custom wrappers for Environment/Console) to be fully testable. Want me to generate those wrappers too?
